s linked to celiac disease\n\nNutritional deficiencies are a common concern for individuals with celiac disease, often arising from inadequate nutrient absorption due to gluten intolerance. The gluten-free diet must be carefully planned to ensure that it addresses these deficiencies, which can lead to serious health complications if not managed properly. The relationship between nutritional deficiencies and the gluten-free diet highlights the importance of dietary planning and monitoring for those affected by celiac disease [Data: Entities (7879, 8394), Relationships (16808)].\n\n## Role of Asociación Española de Celíacos\n\nThe Asociación Española de Celíacos plays a vital role in the community by authorizing foods suitable for a gluten-free diet. This organization helps ensure that patients have access to safe food options, which is critical for their health. The relationship between this organization and the gluten-free diet emphasizes the importance of regulatory bodies in supporting individuals with dietary restrictions [Data: Entities (8392), Relationships (17106)].\n\n## Caloric requirements tailored for celiac patients\n\nCaloric requirements must be adapted for each patient with celiac disease to ensure they receive adequate nutrition while following a gluten-free diet. This personalized approach is essential for maintaining health and preventing further complications. The relationship between caloric requirements and the gluten-free diet underscores the need for individualized dietary planning for those affected by celiac disease [Data: Entities (10458, 8394), Relationships (17121)].\n\n## Integration of ovo-lacto-vegetarian diet with gluten-free needs\n\nThe ovo-lacto-vegetarian diet can be adapted to meet the gluten-free requirements of individuals with gluten intolerance. This flexibility allows for a broader range of dietary options while still adhering to necessary restrictions. The relationship between the ovo-lacto-vegetarian diet and the gluten-free diet highlights the importance of accommodating various dietary preferences within the community of individuals with celiac disease [Data: Entities (8399, 8394), Relationships (17119)].", "# Celiac Patients and Dietary Management\n\nThe community focuses on patients with celiac disease and their dietary needs, particularly the gluten-free diet and low-purine diet. The relationships between these dietary models highlight the complexities of managing multiple dietary restrictions for individuals with celiac disease and related conditions.\n\n## Celiac Patients' Dietary Needs\n\nPatients with celiac disease require a strict gluten-free diet to avoid severe health complications. This dietary restriction is essential for managing their condition and preventing symptoms such as gastrointestinal distress and malnutrition. The relationship between celiac disease and dietary management is crucial, as failure to adhere to these dietary guidelines can lead to significant health risks. [Data: Entities (8396), Relationships (17108)]\n\n## Importance of the Gluten-Free Diet\n\nThe gluten-free diet is a fundamental dietary model for patients with celiac disease. It is designed to eliminate gluten, a protein found in wheat, barley, and rye, which triggers adverse reactions in these patients. The necessity of this diet underscores the importance of proper nutritional guidance and support for individuals diagnosed with celiac disease. [Data: Relationships (6798)]\n\n## Low-Purine Diet for Coexisting Conditions\n\nThe low-purine diet is particularly relevant for patients with celiac disease who also suffer from hyperuricemia or gout. This dietary model aims to limit purine intake, which can exacerbate uric acid levels and lead to painful gout attacks. The intersection of these dietary needs illustrates the complexity of managing multiple health conditions simultaneously. [Data: Entities (8395), Relationships (17124)]\n\n## Artificial Nutrition for Celiac Patients\n\nArtificial nutri